From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elias Bakken <elias@iagent.no>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: recore: drive PD7 low so the board never sources VBUS on USB-C

PD7 (EN-USB-OTG) gates U9, the MT9700 load switch that sources 5V onto
the USB-C connector's VBUS pin.

On Recore A7 there is no pull-down on U9's enable input. A64 pins come
out of reset as high-impedance inputs, so from power-on until something
drives PD7 the enable line floats and the switch may turn itself on -
putting 5V onto VBUS while the port is wired as a peripheral
(usb_otg has dr_mode = "peripheral"). Any attached host is already
driving VBUS, so the two supplies end up fighting across U9. A8 adds
the missing pull-down (R174); A5 and A6 leave PD7 unconnected.

Declaring the rail as a fixed regulator gets it driven low without any
board code. regulators_enable_boot_on() - added for this board in
u-boot-sunxi64-legacy-3-regulators-enable-boot-on - walks the regulator
uclass with uclass_first_device()/uclass_next_device(), and those probe
each device as they go. regulator_autoset() then bails out with
-EMEDIUMTYPE for a regulator that is neither always-on nor boot-on, but
by that point it has already been probed, and probing is what
configures the GPIO: regulator_common_of_to_plat() asks for
GPIOD_IS_OUT, and adds GPIOD_IS_OUT_ACTIVE only when
regulator-boot-on is present. sunxi's set_flags() writes the level
before switching the pin to an output, so there is no glitch high on
the way down.

So the absence of regulator-always-on and regulator-boot-on here is
load-bearing, and deliberately unlike every other regulator in this
file. Adding either one would enable the rail and reintroduce exactly
the conflict this removes.

Driving PD7 low is correct on A7 and harmless on the revisions that
either pull it down already or do not connect it, which matters because
this device tree is shared by all of them.

This shortens the window rather than closing it: the pin still floats
from power-on until u-boot's board_init() runs. Only the pull-down
resistor fixes that completely.

Signed-off-by: Elias Bakken <elias@iagent.no>
---
 arch/arm/dts/sun50i-a64-recore.dts | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm/dts/sun50i-a64-recore.dts b/arch/arm/dts/sun50i-a64-recore.dts
--- a/arch/arm/dts/sun50i-a64-recore.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/dts/sun50i-a64-recore.dts
@@ -102,6 +102,28 @@
 		enable-active-high;
 	};

+	/*
+	 * VBUS source for the USB-C port, gated by PD7 (EN-USB-OTG).
+	 *
+	 * Deliberately has neither regulator-always-on nor
+	 * regulator-boot-on: without them the enable GPIO is requested
+	 * as GPIOD_IS_OUT without GPIOD_IS_OUT_ACTIVE, so probing this
+	 * regulator drives PD7 low instead of high. That is the entire
+	 * point of the node - A7 has no pull-down on the load switch's
+	 * enable input, and a floating enable can make the board source
+	 * 5V into a host that is already driving VBUS.
+	 *
+	 * Do not "fix" this by adding boot-on to match its neighbours.
+	 */
+	reg_usb0_vbus: usb0-vbus {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "usb0-vbus";
+		gpio = <&pio 3 7 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>; /* PD7 */
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <5000000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <5000000>;
+		enable-active-high;
+	};
+
 	reg_hdmi_vbus: hdmi-vbus {
 		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
 		regulator-name = "hdmi-vbus";
